---
import InstrumentBand from "../../../components/lab/instrument/InstrumentBand.astro";
import FissionLight021 from "../../../components/studies/FissionLight021.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
  (entry) => entry.slug === "fission-light-2026-11-11",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section>
    <h2 id="problem">01 / A push, a wait, and a borrowed beam</h2>
    <p>
      A fission engine, an unsanctioned part, does not thrust steadily. While
      held it pushes, waits and pushes again: today 0.18 seconds of thrust in
      every 0.45. Releasing cuts a push short and does not shorten the wait. One
      push is about 1.9 times a Light ion engine’s thrust, but averaged over the
      cycle the engine delivers less than a Light ion. The sense of a very big
      push has to come from how each push looks.
    </p>
    <p>
      In flight the light shows none of this. Each push paints an amber beam in
      the Light ion beam’s shape, the light of an engine with no design of its
      own, and the wait adds no light, so the beam fades through it. A held
      fission engine looks like an ion engine that flickers.
    </p>
    <p>
      The hypothesis is that each push is an explosion behind the ship, in a
      nuclear green near the yellow-green already on the engine’s body. The
      tension is between size and readability. The explosion has to look like
      the cause of the push on a Wasp or Scout without burying the ship, and its
      green has to stay apart from the mint of Light ion and stabilizer light,
      the cyan of Heavy ion and the amber of plasma.
    </p>

    <h3>Held fixed</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Fission is pulsed: its thrust arrives as separate pushes with a wait
        between them.
      </li>
      <li>
        The engine body, the ship drawings, and ion and plasma light as they
        are.
      </li>
      <li>
        The three canvas layers: World for what is solid, persistent FX light
        and fresh LFX light. Fission’s light goes on FX, LFX or both.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Left open</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>What one push looks like, and its colour.</li>
      <li>What the wait shows, if anything.</li>
      <li>Which layer carries each.</li>
      <li>Whether the look holds at a slow rhythm and at a fast one.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Outside this study</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The rhythm itself. The expectation is about one push every 0.9 seconds
        while held, with tapping able to force a couple of pushes a second. That
        is flight behaviour and needs its own decision; the examples make the
        rhythm adjustable so the look can be judged at both.
      </li>
      <li>
        Fission’s thrust, mass, speed contribution and energy draw, and moving
        it into scored play.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <aside class="stamp" aria-labelledby="stamp">
      <h3 id="stamp">Question check · 11 November 2026</h3>
      <p>
        A reviewer with no part in the framing tested this section as first
        written. The section was then corrected; the corrected text has not been
        tested.
        <a href="/lab/reviews/fission-light-2026-11-11"
          >Full result and findings</a
        >
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Metric</th>
            <th scope="col">Result</th>
            <th scope="col">Reason</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">One subject</th>
            <td>Pass with findings</td>
            <td>
              One object, the fission engine’s light, in its push and its wait
              and beside ion and plasma light. No open study owns part of the
              answer. The title and question stated the explosion as settled.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Grounded</th>
            <td>Pass with findings</td>
            <td>
              Every number and behaviour checked out, and every open item can be
              shown and judged by looking. The layer rule as worded was in no
              specification, and several descriptions were imprecise.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Consequence</th>
            <td>Pass</td>
            <td>
              A fission engine paints a light of its own in flight, where today
              it paints the amber beam and adds nothing in the wait.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="detonation">02.1 / A detonation left behind</h2>
    <p>
      The first example answers the question with the most literal explosion.
      Each push goes off at a point 12 units behind the nozzle and stays at that
      point in the world while the ship is thrown away from it. The plasma ball
      and the ion beam both travel with the ship; this light does not.
    </p>
    <p>
      A blast has three parts, all stamped into the persistent FX light: a
      white-hot flash about two Wasp lengths across that fades in six ticks, a
      thin shock ring that steps outward on every second tick so the fading
      light keeps separate rings, and fourteen hard sparks thrown back in a
      cone. The green is the engine body’s yellow-green, from a pale hot centre
      to a deeper green edge. The wait between pushes paints nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
      The scene opens inside the first push, with a push every 0.9 seconds.
      Press Play to watch three pushes and a coast. In the menu in the top-right
      corner, the slider sets how often the engine pushes, down to the game’s
      0.45 seconds and below, and the Detonation switch shows the game’s amber
      beam on the same flight.
    </p>
  </section>
  <InstrumentBand><FissionLight021 client:visible /></InstrumentBand>
  <section>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The slider changes this scene’s flight only. A slower rhythm also means
        less thrust over time, so the ship is slower.
      </li>
      <li>
        The scene cannot show tapping: the instrument’s burns hold an input for
        a second or longer. A burn of short presses is a missing capability of
        the Flight scene instrument.
      </li>
      <li>
        The scene plays a fixed burn, so it shows how a push looks. How it feels
        under the hand needs the game.
      </li>
      <li>Sizes, colours, counts and durations are working values.</li>
      <li>
        Reference conditions: a Wasp with a Fission main engine and ion
        stabilizers, at rest at the run start, burn B1, the Follow view, no
        randomness.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section><h2>03 / To be developed after 02</h2></section>
</StudyLayout>

<style>
/* The prose column's width; the stamp's own type is smaller. */
.stamp {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  max-width: 41rem;
  margin-top: var(--spacing-lg);
  padding: var(--spacing-md) var(--spacing-lg);
  border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
  font-size: var(--font-size-caption);
}
.stamp table {
  border-collapse: collapse;
}
.stamp th,
.stamp td {
  padding: var(--spacing-sm) var(--spacing-md) var(--spacing-sm) 0;
  text-align: left;
  vertical-align: top;
}
.stamp td:not(:last-child) {
  white-space: nowrap;
}
.stamp tbody th {
  white-space: nowrap;
  font-weight: normal;
  color: var(--color-text);
}
</style>
